WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.269
"Hospitals Profit on Junk Food"

00:00:11.269 --> 00:00:15.880
Put in stark terms, cardiovascular
disease claims one American life

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:23.259
every 39 seconds, and for most heart
attack deaths, you just keel over.

00:00:23.259 --> 00:00:29.009
Sudden cardiac death is the first
manifestation of coronary heart disease

00:00:29.009 --> 00:00:33.160
for the majority of individuals,
particularly among women.

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:38.660
So for many of these sudden
death victims, their first indication

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:43.300
of the presence of heart disease was
their demise. They didn't even know

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:46.900
they had heart disease! That's why an
ounce of prevention is worth way more

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:51.730
than a pound of cure, because
there is no cure for dead.

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:55.560
But that's why the prevention of sudden
cardiac death remains such a major

00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:00.100
public health challenge, because most
people don't even know they're at risk.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:04.360
But we've known for over a half century
when we first started autopsying

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:07.970
young servicemen who died in the
Korean war that coronary artery

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:13.990
disease begins in our youth,
even among young children.

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:19.190
So business as usual is simply not going
to yield the improvements necessary

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:24.220
to radically improve the cardiovascular
health of the United States.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:29.479
But the good news is a low-risk lifestyle—
not smoking, exercising regularly,

00:01:29.479 --> 00:01:32.540
having a prudent diet, and maintaining
a healthy weight—may be able

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:36.690
to wipe out the vast majority
of risk for sudden cardiac death.

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:42.210
So the time is now long overdue to start
aggressive preventive cardiovascular

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:45.940
disease programs in our schools,
our homes, our worksites.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:52.060
How about starting in our hospitals?
A significant percentage of hospitals

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:58.970
surveyed had fast food restaurants inside
them, with Krispy Kreme topping the list.

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:03.420
Brilliant marketing, given that families
surveyed in hospitals with McDonald's

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:06.300
were twice as likely to think
McDonald's was healthy,

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:10.128
after all, they let it in a hospital.

00:02:10.128 --> 00:02:14.660
But what about food served in
hospital cafeterias themselves?

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:21.090
Are they much better? 384 entrees
were analyzed at 14 children's hospitals

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:26.430
in California and only 7%
could be classified as healthy.

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:31.090
And just in case someone accidentally
picked the rare healthy option,

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:35.640
81% of eating venues in children's
hospitals made sure to have junky

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:41.440
impulse buys like ice cream, cookies,
and candy near the checkout register.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:46.540
And 38% had signs that explicitly
encouraged unhealthy eating.

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:49.350
Why would they do that?

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:53.959
If you ask hospital cafeteria
managers that exact question,

00:02:53.959 --> 00:02:57.460
why they don't tend to
follow nutrition standards,

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:03.560
why nutrition is not a top priority, it's
the same answer why unhealthy food

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:10.130
is sold anywhere else: the
pressure to generate profit.

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:14.370
Increased emphasis is being placed
on running a hospital foodservice

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:19.930
department as a profit center,
a bigger and bigger profit center.

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:24.330
It's such a metaphor for our
sickness-care system in general,

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:28.440
where healthy treat-the-cause
approaches are eclipsed

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:31.810
by the pills and procedures
that bring in the most money.

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:35.560
Well, what do you expect
from the private sector?

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:38.500
But public hospitals
don't seem much better.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:43.090
A 2019 analysis of veterans' hospitals
found that all VA hospitals

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:47.590
contain vending machines providing a
majority of soda, candy, and junk foods

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:50.629
that directly conflict with our
government's own healthy food choice

00:03:50.629 --> 00:03:55.450
recommendations such that, ironically,
hospital visits could theoretically

00:03:55.450 --> 00:03:59.440
promote worse health. This raises
the question why any soda

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:02.280
or candy machines are 
available our VA hospitals?

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:07.390
Are we trading the health
of our veterans for profits?

00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:11.280
Maybe it's time to ban junk
food on hospital premises.

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:15.209
On daily rounds it is appalling to see
patients gorging on potato chips,

00:04:15.209 --> 00:04:18.039
candy, and soda—the very foods
that may have contributed

00:04:18.039 --> 00:04:20.269
to their admission
in the first place.

00:04:20.269 --> 00:04:26.140
Obscene to see fast food
franchises on site, legitimizing

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:30.130
the acceptability and consumption
of such foods in the daily diet.

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:34.180
The obesity epidemic
represents a public health crisis,

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:38.580
but it's a public health scandal
that by legitimizing junk food,

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:43.020
hospitals have themselves become
a risk factor for diet-related disease.

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:48.220
Maybe it's time to stop
selling sickness in the hospital.

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:52.240
What message do residents receive
when they are fed pizza and soda pop

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:54.540
at grand rounds on obesity?

00:04:54.540 --> 00:05:08.000
We need a healthcare system with
more Hippocrates and less hypocrisy.

